{"chunk_id": "683d0daf8616", "text": "In this section, Yudhishthira and Krishna discuss the grim logic of conflict with the Kauravas. The text reflects on how enmity perpetuates itself\u2014hatred feeds hatred like fire fed with clarified butter\u2014and suggests that true peace between deeply hostile rivals is almost impossible unless one side is decisively defeated. Yudhishthira nevertheless hopes to preserve both virtue and political interest and asks Krishna\u2019s counsel. Krishna agrees to go as an envoy to the Kuru court, attempting peace while recognizing Duryodhana\u2019s stubbornness and power, supported by great warriors like Bhishma and Drona. Krishna argues that while peace is desirable, a Kshatriya\u2019s duty ultimately allows only victory or death in battle. The passage explores the moral tension between righteousness, diplomacy, honor, and the inevitability of war.\nYudhishthira debates peace versus war with Krishna, who offers to seek peace but warns that Kshatriya duty may require battle against Duryodhana.\nThemes: peace vs war, kshatriya duty, inevitability of conflict, diplomacy before battle, loyalty and counsel", "vector": [0.002297930885106325, -0.018263032659888268, -0.002981958445161581, 0.01109161414206028, 0.0011180253932252526, 0.026973092928528786, -0.019320013001561165, -0.004733001813292503, -0.02519361861050129, -0.022785305976867676, 0.028765948489308357, 0.00853612832725048, 0.019708018749952316, 0.0016481884522363544, -0.016282863914966583, 0.012008110992610455, 0.05207305774092674, -0.00880371779203415, 0.037382353097200394, -0.02241067960858345, -0.0014658926520496607, -0.004308202303946018, -0.004144303500652313, 0.004880176391452551, -0.011058165691792965, 0.04128916934132576, 0.018370067700743675, -0.02519361861050129, -0.004431962966918945, -0.022397300228476524, -0.004900245927274227, -0.003227806882932782, 0.0029970104806125164, -0.008261848241090775, -0.02283882535994053, -0.002117307623848319, -0.0011932851048186421, -0